3240966796875, -0.01177215576171875, -0.032928466796875, 0.0085296630859375, 0.0284881591796875, -0.0011806488037109375, 0.0213775634765625, 0.016845703125, -0.0208587646484375, -0.01349639892578125, 0.0272674560546875, 0.009918212890625, 0.033599853515625, 0.001094818115234375, 0.00992584228515625, -0.01314544677734375, 0.042327880859375, 0.0135955810546875, 0.003597259521484375, 0.0165863037109375, -0.0259857177734375, -0.0153656005859375, 0.003360748291015625, -0.01552581787109375, -0.0195159912109375, 0.032318115234375, -0.0029811859130859375, 0.039215087890625, -0.0264892578125, -0.003448486328125, 0.0011491775512695312, 0.0004892349243164062, 0.0205535888671875, -0.018402099609375, 0.0007443428039550781, -0.0218505859375, -0.026336669921875, 0.05035400390625, -0.0218963623046875, -0.0087432861328125, 0.0126800537109375, 6.29425048828125e-05, 0.0160369873046875, 0.01529693603515625, 0.038360595703125, 0.03765869140625, -0.0104827880859375, -0.00655364990234375, -0.0012950897216796875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose: To develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance.\n\nMaterials and Methods:\n\nDataset: This retrospective study included 638 radiology reports from CT examinations of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists in 2023 and 2024.\n\nAI System Development: A locally deployed multi-agent AI system was developed, consisting of specialized agents with distinct roles. The structuring agent segmented reports into standardized anatomical sections at the sentence level, using a combination of regular expression (regex) rules\u2014designed to identify anatomical terms and section headers\u2014and local large language models to ensure accurate classification. The quality assurance (QA) agents detected mismatches between the Findings and Impression sections, inconsistencies within sections, gender-anatomy conflicts, and undocumented communication of critical findings. Agents communicated sequentially, with the output of the structuring agent serving as input for the QA agents.\n\nEvaluation Process: Two board-certified radiologists independently evaluated a subset of 45 reports that had been restructured and flagged by the system. Evaluation criteria included: (1) accuracy of anatomical structuring, (2) retention of original clinical content, (3) absence of fabricated information, and (4) overall QA performance rated as \"excellent,\" \"good,\" or \"fair.\"\n\nResults: The multi-agent AI system structured the Findings sections of all 638 reports (22,270 sentences) into a predefined anatomical format while retaining the original report content. The system flagged 90 reports (14.1% of 638), most commonly for section mismatches (80 reports, 12.5%). In the radiologist evaluation of 45 reports, both reviewers agreed that 31 (69%) were correctly restructured, 2 (4%) were incorrectly restructured, and disagreed on 12 (27%). Both reviewers agreed that no clinically important information was omitted and no fabricated content was introduced. Overall QA performance was rated as \"excellent\" or \"good\" in 38 of 45 reports (84%), with the remaining 7 reports (16%) rated as \"fair.\"\n\nConclusion: A locally deployed multi-agent AI system combined radiology report structuring and quality assurance within a single workflow, demonstrating favorable performance in radiologist evaluation. Limitations include the retrospective design and use of a single-institution dataset. Future work may explore prospective validation, broader datasets, and further refinement of agent collaboration. Such systems may support standardization of reporting and quality assurance in radiology practice."}}